### Treat standalone carriage returns as stderr line terminators

**Severity:** `medium`

A progress-style stderr stream can use bare carriage returns without line feeds. The current branch drops the terminator and keeps appending to the same buffered line, so unrelated progress updates can merge into one log entry and consume the byte budget unexpectedly.

```suggestion
        if (value == (byte)'\r')
        {
            EmitLine();
            ResetLine();
            return;
        }
```

### Bound package.json before reading and deserializing it

**Severity:** `high`

An extension package controls `package.json`, so reading it with `File.ReadAllText` allows an oversized manifest to allocate memory before validation can reject it. Put a byte limit at the file boundary and return a parse failure when the manifest is too large.

```suggestion
        const int MaxPackageJsonBytes = 1024 * 1024;
        string json;
        try
        {
            using var stream = new FileStream(packageJsonPath, FileMode.Open, FileAccess.Read, FileShare.Read, bufferSize: 4096, FileOptions.SequentialScan);
            if (stream.Length > MaxPackageJsonBytes)
            {
                return JSExtensionManifestParseResult.Failure($"The package.json exceeds the {MaxPackageJsonBytes}-byte limit.");
            }

            using var reader = new StreamReader(stream, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, detectEncodingFromByteOrderMarks: true);
            json = reader.ReadToEnd();
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException)
        {
            return JSExtensionManifestParseResult.Failure($"Failed to read '{packageJsonPath}': {ex.Message}");
        }

        if (System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount(json) > MaxPackageJsonBytes)
        {
            return JSExtensionManifestParseResult.Failure($"The package.json exceeds the {MaxPackageJsonBytes}-byte limit.");
        }

        var directory = Path.GetDirectoryName(Path.GetFullPath(packageJsonPath)) ?? string.Empty;
        return TryParse(json, directory);
```

### Use ordinal containment checks for case-sensitive directories

**Severity:** `high`

The text containment check uses a case-insensitive prefix comparison. On a case-sensitive Windows directory, an entry point such as `../foo/index.js` can escape from package directory `Foo` into a distinct sibling while still matching the prefix text.

```suggestion
        var prefix = baseDirectory.EndsWith(Path.DirectorySeparatorChar)
            ? baseDirectory
            : baseDirectory + Path.DirectorySeparatorChar;

        if (!resolved.StartsWith(prefix, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            error = $"The entry point '{entryPoint}' must not escape the extension directory.";
            return null;
        }
```

### Fail closed while walking the resolved manifest path

**Severity:** `high`

The reparse-point walk should use the same ordinal semantics as the prefix check and should not trust a path segment that disappears during validation. Returning `false` for a missing segment lets validation succeed without proving that every segment remained inside the extension directory.

```suggestion
            var baseDirectory = Path.TrimEndingDirectorySeparator(Path.GetFullPath(extensionDirectory));
            var current = Path.GetFullPath(resolvedEntryPoint);

            // Walk from the entry point up toward the extension directory. The extension directory
            // itself and everything above it are outside this check.
            while (!string.Equals(Path.TrimEndingDirectorySeparator(current), baseDirectory, StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                if (IsReparsePoint(current))
                {
                    error = $"The entry point '{resolvedEntryPoint}' traverses a symbolic link or junction, which is not allowed.";
                    return false;
                }

                var parent = Path.GetDirectoryName(current);
                if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(parent) || string.Equals(parent, current, StringComparison.Ordinal))
                {
                    // Reached a filesystem root without meeting the extension directory. The text
                    // containment check already ran, so this only happens for pathological inputs.
                    break;
                }

                current = parent;
            }

            return true;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException or ArgumentException or NotSupportedException or PathTooLongException or System.Security.SecurityException)
        {
            error = $"The entry point '{resolvedEntryPoint}' could not be validated: {ex.Message}";
            return false;
        }
    }

    private static bool IsReparsePoint(string path)
    {
        try
        {
            return (File.GetAttributes(path) & FileAttributes.ReparsePoint) == FileAttributes.ReparsePoint;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is FileNotFoundException or DirectoryNotFoundException)
        {
            return true;
        }
```

### Avoid partial notification frames on caller cancellation

**Severity:** `high`

The cancellation token is documented as canceling before the send starts, but it is passed into the actual frame write. If cancellation fires mid-write, the output stream can contain a partial JSON-RPC frame while the connection remains reusable, so the next message may be parsed incorrectly.

```suggestion
        ObjectDisposedException.ThrowIf(Volatile.Read(ref _disposed) != 0, this);
        ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(method);
        cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();

        try
        {
            var notification = _messageFactory.CreateRequestMessage();
            notification.Method = method;
            notification.Arguments = parameters;
            await _messageHandler.WriteAsync(notification, _connectionClosedCts.Token).ConfigureAwait(false);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (_connectionClosedCts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            DisposeRpc();
            throw new JsonRpcException("The JSON-RPC connection failed while writing a notification.");
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is ConnectionLostException or ObjectDisposedException or IOException or TimeoutException)
        {
            DisposeRpc();
            throw new JsonRpcException("The JSON-RPC connection failed while writing a notification.", ex);
        }
```

### Serialize RPC method registration side effects

**Severity:** `high`

`ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd` can execute its factory more than once for the same key. Because this factory calls `AddLocalRpcMethod`, concurrent registrations for the same method can attempt duplicate JSON-RPC target registration even though only one value is retained.

```suggestion
    private void RegisterMethod(string method)
    {
        lock (_registeredMethods)
        {
            if (_registeredMethods.ContainsKey(method))
            {
                return;
            }

            var target = new RpcMethodTarget(this, method);
            _rpc.AddLocalRpcMethod(
                typeof(RpcMethodTarget).GetMethod(nameof(RpcMethodTarget.InvokeAsync))!,
                target,
                new JsonRpcMethodAttribute(method)
                {
                    UseSingleObjectParameterDeserialization = true,
                });
            _registeredMethods[method] = target;
        }
    }
```

### Do not synchronously wait for JSON-RPC pumps during Dispose

**Severity:** `medium`

A request or notification handler can call `Dispose()` while the corresponding pump task is still executing that handler. Waiting synchronously on `_notificationConsumerTask` or `_rpc.Completion` from inside that path stalls shutdown for the timeout and can make extension teardown appear hung. Signal cancellation and let the existing continuation dispose the token sources after the pump tasks really complete.

```suggestion
        var tasks = new[]
        {
            _notificationConsumerTask ?? Task.CompletedTask,
            _errorPumpTask ?? Task.CompletedTask,
            _rpc.Completion,
        };
        _ = DisposeTokenSourcesWhenTasksCompleteAsync(tasks, _disposalCts, _connectionClosedCts);
```
